    "lawCode": "Amd §202, St Ad Proc Act",
    "lawSection": "State Administrative Procedure Act",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S6916\n\nSPONSOR: JACOBS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the state administrative procedure act, in relation to\nincreasing the revised rule public comment period from thirty days to\nforty-five days\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to amend the State Administrative Procedure\nAct (SAPA), to allow more time to comment on proposed rules that agen-\ncies have revised.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends the State Administrative Procedure Act (SAPA) Section\n202, subdivision 4-a, requiring state agencies to allow for 45-days,\ninstead of 30-days, public comment when the agency revises a rule\nproposal.\n\nSection 2: establishes the effective date.\n\n \nEXISTING LAW:\n\nState Administrative Procedure Act 202-4a\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nRegulated entities and legislators have complained that the revised rule\nmaking 30-day comment period, currently required, is too short of a\nperiod to review and properly comment on agency revised rulemakings. The\nAdministrative Regulations Review Commission has received comments stat-\ning that, at least, a 45-day comment period should be allowed when an\nagency decides to make substantive revisions to a proposed rule making.\n\nThe Department of Environmental Conservation (DEC) recently proposed,\nrevised and adopted substantial changes to the rules that implement the\nsolid waste program in New York. The rules affected landfills, mulch\nfacilities, recycling businesses, waste tire operations, metal process-\ning, vehicle dismantling, combustion facilities and oil processing. In\nshort, Thousands of business owners were affected. DEC received more\nthan 5000 public comments, so many that Senators' Catharine Young and\nChris Jacobs and Assemblymembers' Steve Englebright and Aravella Simotas\nformally requested DEC to extend the original 45-day public comment\nperiod. DEC graciously extended the comment period to 180 days since the\nDepartment had received thousands of comments. The Department then\nrevised the rule making, taking into account many of the comments. The\nrevised rulemaking was issued and commenced a 30-day public comment\nperiod required by SAPA 202-4a.  Even though DEC only had to wait 30\nmore days, the Department again graciously accepted revised comments for\na longer period. The rule was adopted into law on September 20, 2017.\n\nThis example is a best-case scenario; the Agency listened to state\nlegislators, businesses and environmentalists asking for more time to\ndigest this very important rulemaking and DEC even allowed for more time\nthan statutorily allowed on the revised rulemaking comment period of\n30-days. However, many agencies merely abide by the SAPA requirements of\na 45-day comment period for proposed rules and a 30-day comment period\nfor revised rules.\n\nThis legislation is necessary to change the statutory time frame for\nrevised rule makings from the current 30-day window to a full 45-days so\nthat businesses, environmentalists and the general public have ample\ntime to make competent comments on revised rules. A 30-day comment peri-\nod is simply too short and it should be increased to 45-days.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
